💭 What proxies say of a request they pass on, in the X-Forwarded- headers, and how much
💭 of it is believed. A proxy adds what it saw to the end of each header, so the entries
💭 of proxies that are the server's own are the last ones, and everything before them
💭 may have been written by the client.
🐇 🛰 🍇
  📗
    What *header*, the value of an X-Forwarded- header with all its lines
    taken together, is believed to say when *hops* proxies in front of the
    server are its own: the entry that many places from the right, or the
    leftmost when there are fewer. An entry that is not what it should be
    ends the walk there, and the one before it stands. The empty text when
    nothing is believed. *kind* says what an entry should be: 0 an address,
    1 a scheme (http or https), 2 a host with or without a port, 3 the
    prefix of a path.
